\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Workers Ready for Union Revote --- Labor board to start sending ballots Friday to warehouse employees in Alabama\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 895 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . workers at a company warehouse in Alabama are set to begin voting for the second time in the past year on whether to unionize.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  on Friday will start to send ballots to roughly 6,100 workers at the facility in Bessemer, Ala. In recent weeks, organizers and managers at the facility clashed over election procedures and company policies. The ballots must reach the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  regional office by March 25.\par \par \uc2 Last year about 71% of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers who voted decided against unionization, an overwhelming victory for the company. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  found \uc2 Amazon\uc2  violated labor law during the vote and late last year ordered a new election.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said it didn't violate labor law and it has worked hard to listen to its employees and offer "great pay and benefits in a safe and inclusive workplace." The company said this week that it recognizes the right of employees to vote to join a union and aims to provide information to workers on the potential impact of unionization. In the past, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  told workers that such a step is unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 As the second vote nears, organizers say the primary difference this time is the high vaccination rate among the U.S. public, which they said made it easier to meet with workers. During the previous election, the union communicated largely through mail brochures and phone calls.\par \par \uc2 Kristina Bell, a Bessemer worker and organizer, said employees are now generally more knowledgeable about unions and their goals because of the first vote's publicity, making it easier to talk to voters about the potential of joining a union.\par \par \uc2 So far the group hasn't had as much star-studded pizazz as in 2021. Last year's vote attracted support from Democratic leaders such as Stacey Abrams, National Football League players, and actors that included Danny Glover, who visited the Bessemer facility. President Biden weighed in, calling the election a "vitally important choice" in a video last February.\par \par \uc2 The union embraced the publicity as a way to help connect with workers during the outbreak, said \uc2 Stuart Appelbaum\uc2 , president of Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union, which has led the campaign in Bessemer. Organizers advocated for higher pay, more breaks and eased performance quotas for workers.\par \par \uc2 Outside support "is not as necessary this time to be able to communicate with workers," Mr. Appelbaum said. "We can do it more directly now."\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said it opposes unions because it prefers to negotiate with workers directly. The company held meetings at the Bessemer center to discourage workers from unionizing. Since the last election \uc2 Amazon\uc2  raised wages and introduced bonuses of up to $3,000. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's starting wage averages $18 an hour, and the company recently said it eased some of its requirements for the pace at which workers must sort or prepare packages.\par \par \uc2 Among the challenges facing the RWDSU is convincing workers about the benefits provided by unions. After the first election, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers who voted against unionization said they didn't believe it would substantially improve their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's employee turnover presents a hurdle. The RWDSU estimates that nearly half of the 6,143 eligible voters for this year's election weren't employees at the Bessemer facility when the first vote took place. The company has had turnover that exceeds 100% in many of its facilities, according to an analysis by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 The revote is happening after the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  determined in November that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  broke the law during the first election, which was also held by mail, primarily because of a mailbox the company had installed in front of the facility. A labor-board official said the box could have made voters believe the company played a role in counting the votes.\par \par \uc2 Union supporters and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  have disputed actions the company has taken as the vote approaches. The union has again raised concern about the mailbox, which was moved from the front of the building but is still present at the Bessemer campus. It recently filed a complaint against \uc2 Amazon\uc2  after the company allegedly gave a written warning to a worker who organized inside the Bessemer facility.\par \par \uc2 An Amazon spokeswoman said the company's letter acknowledged the worker's right to organize while pointing out the company's policy of not doing so while interrupting other people's work.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  recently agreed to notify past and current warehouse staff by email and other internal communications of their rights to organize in its buildings under a deal with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 . The agreement suggests that workers organize when they aren't working.\par \par \uc2 Workers might have their best leverage in years, labor researchers have said. A national labor shortage has given employees across industries bargaining advantages to push for higher pay and benefits. This trend contributed to a unionization push at \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 . and strikes at \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 ., the snack producer Mondelez International Inc. and Kellogg Co.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  said it has focused on worker flexibility while introducing such perks as paid college tuition.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has shown signs of feeling the labor crunch. The company said it would spend roughly $4 billion in the three months ended in December to address costs associated with getting products to customers.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220203ei230000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
