\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 December Jobs Showed Resilience to Omicron\b0\par\par\uc2 By Justin Lahart \par \uc2 471 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 What happened with the job market in December is in many respects old news. It just isn't as old as it used to be.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department on Tuesday said there were a seasonally adjusted 10.93 million job openings at the end of December, up from 10.78 million in November and close to the 11.09 million notched in October. That amounts to 1.7 open jobs for every person classified as unemployed in December.\par \par \uc2 The report also showed employers made 6.23 million hires in December, while separations -- people leaving their jobs because they quit, were fired and so on -- came to 5.9 million. The difference of 363,000 between those two figures equates to how many jobs the economy added during the month.\par \par \uc2 But wait, didn't we already learn what job growth looked like in the final month of the year from the December employment report? Well, sort of.\par \par \uc2 The job figures in the employment report are based on the pay period during the week that includes the 12th of the month, which basically makes it a midmonth reading. The turnover figures the Labor Department reported Tuesday cover the entire month. (The opening and turnover figures used to be reported after the following month's employment figures; this year the Labor Department started releasing them earlier.)\par \par \uc2 So Tuesday's report contains a couple more weeks' data than the December jobs report, which might not be particularly informative in usual times, but perhaps is more meaningful considering that the latter half of December was when the Omicron variant really took hold. In that mid-December week the monthly job figures were based on, there were an average of about 132,000 new Covid-19 cases reported each day, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 . In the final week of December, the average daily case count was about 394,000.\par \par \uc2 What is striking is that December's addition of a net 363,000 jobs in Tuesday's report isn't far off the previous month's 391,000. Even with Omicron, employers kept on adding workers.\par \par \uc2 The extent to which that will carry over to the January employment report, which the Labor Department will release Friday, is uncertain. The week last month's job tally will be based on happens to be the one in which Covid-19 peaked, with an average of about 804,000 cases a day. Sickened workers and job seekers, coupled with disruptions to schools and other services, could weigh on the numbers.\par \par \uc2 Still, the fact employers kept on hiring in December, despite everything, counts for something. If Omicron knocked the job market down last month, it will probably get right back up again.\par \par \uc2 page,5043 \par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220202ei220002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
