\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Flexible Hours Are What Workers Want\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katherine Bindley \par \uc2 789 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 January 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers, flush with power as employers raise wages and scramble to fill open jobs, say they care even more about flexible schedules than whether or not they go into an office.\par \par \uc2 Ninety-five percent of people surveyed want flexible hours, compared with 78% of workers who want location flexibility, according to a new report from Future Forum, a consortium focused on reimagining the future of work led by \uc2 Slack Technologies Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The new data, collected in November 2021 from a survey of more than 10,000 knowledge workers, offers a snapshot into just how popular hybrid arrangements have become in the second year of the pandemic, how virtually all workers prize schedule flexibility above all and the growing concerns that many bosses have about how to keep promotions and pay fair when some employees are in the office while others stay home.\par \par \uc2 The survey also found that 72% of workers who weren't happy with their level of flexibility -- whether time or location -- are likely to seek out a new opportunity in the next year.\par \par \uc2 "If they're not getting what they want, they're open to looking for a new job," said Sheela Subramanian, vice president of the Future Forum.\par \par \uc2 Many employers have reluctantly embraced long-term hybrid and remote work arrangements after repeatedly postponing return-to-office dates or finding that workers pushed back on going to the office. That has some executives thinking differently about in-person arrangements.\par \par \uc2 Maeve O'Meara\uc2 , chief executive officer of San Francisco healthcare-technology company Castlight Health Inc., said her employees should gather only when there's a specific need to do so. "We should really be organizing around bringing people together for an explicit purpose, whether that's collaboration, innovation, planning or just socializing," she said.\par \par \uc2 Agreements between team members about when people in the group will work are growing in popularity across industries, said Ms. Subramanian. Flexible schedules are likely to endure beyond the pandemic, she said.\par \par \uc2 Focusing on how many hours people are working is outdated, she said: "It really needs to be a shift from presenteeism and activity tracking to actually understanding the results that people are driving and the value that they're creating."\par \par \uc2 The Future Forum survey, which was conducted between Nov. 1 and Nov. 30, also found that the share of people working in hybrid models, where they split their time between an office and a remote location, increased by 12 percentage points since May, as more workers have returned part time to their traditional workplaces. More than two-thirds of those surveyed said a hybrid setup was their preferred way of working.\par \par \uc2 While many large companies have decided that the majority of their employees will combine remote work with in-office days, hybrid work has its downsides. Executives have growing concerns that hybrid work could increase inequity among rank-and-file employees, especially women, working mothers and people of color, who when surveyed said they were more likely to prefer flexible arrangements.\par \par \uc2 Among executives surveyed, 71% said they work in the office at least three days a week; 63% of nonexecutive employees said they go in as often. There's a sharper divide between their preferences. Executives working remotely were far more likely than nonexecutives to say they want to work at least three days a week in the office, at 75% versus 37% of employees.\par \par \uc2 What employees want may not be the most effective way for organizations to operate, said \uc2 Nicholas Bloom\uc2 , a professor of economics at Stanford University who researches remote work. "There is going to be a battle royal over choice versus coordination," he said.\par \par \uc2 In a forthcoming survey that Mr. Bloom conducted, some initial results align with Future Forum's employee sentiment on the importance of flexibility. Seventy-five percent of those surveyed by Mr. Bloom want to choose the days they work from home, as opposed to their employer telling them which days to go in.\par \par \uc2 "Firms are going to be reluctant to force employees to coordinate," Mr. Bloom said. "It's not going to go well because on any given day 20% are going to be at home."\par \par \uc2 Large meetings are harder to conduct if some people are in office and some are remote, he said. People inside companies complain about the lack of energy in the workplace when it is sparsely populated. Forcing a one-size-fits-all solution across a large workforce can seem risky to managers, he said, at a time many workers are a flight risk.\par \par \uc2 "They're going to feel like they're going to have to let them choose," Mr. Bloom said of companies.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Chip Cutter contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220126ei1q0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
