\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- The Worker's Moment --- Wages are rising as companies scramble to hire enough people to keep up with demand. In sectors like manufacturing, just about anyone who wants a job can get one. Here's what the American labor landscape looks like now.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Chaney Cambon and Andrew Mollica \par \uc2 322 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's a good time to be an American worker. Wages are climbing higher, and they're expected to stay that way for years. Employees can quit a job and likely find better pay elsewhere. Many companies are boosting salaries as they scramble to fill open positions.\par \par \uc2 Most workers are benefiting from a tight labor market that developed during the nation's recovery from Covid-19. Those in highest demand are in some cases the ones who started out further behind, with fewer gains before the pandemic. They include the young and the less educated, as well as those who work in lower-wage industries and perform blue-collar tasks. Many are based in the South. Wealthier, older, college-educated professionals in other parts of the country are also making gains.\par \par \uc2 This shift in bargaining power is the latest example of how the pandemic upended the U.S. economy in unexpected ways. The speed of the country's recovery has already replaced nearly 85% of the 22 million jobs lost during the first wave of the virus. The comeback was the result of government stimulus, easing of lockdowns, consumers who kept spending and rising demand for certain products and services. Jobless claims rose for the week ended Jan. 15, but economists don't expect that to persist.\par \par \uc2 Millions of workers decided during the pandemic to quit, retire or leave the workforce altogether. That made it tougher for companies to fill roles. Today, just about anyone who wants a job can get one. Here's a by-the-numbers look at today's labor landscape.\par \par \uc2 (See accompanying illustration -- WSJ Jan. 22, 2022)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220122ei1m0002e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
