\par\par \b \uc2 Outlook 2022 (A Special Report) --- Workers Relish A Feeling of Power: With companies scrambling to fill jobs, employees are enjoying newfound leverage\b0\par\par\uc2 By Te-Ping Chen \par \uc2 1,007 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 January 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Raises, signing bonuses, more flexibility. Amid a historically tight labor market, more workers are feeling their power -- and flexing it.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. clocked 4.5 million worker resignations in November, the highest quit rate on record. As the economy continues to bounce back, workers are feeling empowered to seek new opportunities, knowing that they are more in demand than ever.\par \par \uc2 "People are realizing this is the one chance in a long time when employees seem to have some leverage," says Andrew McGregor, a 41-year-old IT security professional in Tampa, Fla. Last year, Mr. McGregor decided he would leave his job if he could get a big enough raise to make it worth his while. Shortly thereafter, he landed an offer for 30% more than he was making, and he quit.\par \par \uc2 More than 80% of American workers say the pandemic has given them more clarity about their personal and professional goals, according to a recent survey by human-resources-services company \uc2 Randstad NV\uc2 . They also have higher expectations for their employers, with 77% saying they want more job flexibility.\par \par \uc2 "I refuse to accept a position that's just a position," says Micah Worcester, age 27, of Oklahoma City, who quit her retail job last fall, fed up with being mistreated by customers. "I'm looking for something that I actually want to do," she says.\par \par \uc2 Particularly in hard-hit sectors such as retail and hospitality, where labor shortages have forced businesses to close or shorten hours, employers are boosting wages and offering new perks, such as healthcare and education benefits, in a bid to attract workers. Average hourly wages in December jumped 4.7% over the previous year, well above the roughly 3% pre-pandemic wage-growth rate.\par \par \uc2 "The market is red hot and there's a lot of inflation when it comes to compensation," says \uc2 Paul Knopp\uc2 , U.S. chairman and chief executive of KPMG LLP, a professional-services firm. \uc2 KPMG\uc2 , which has 34,000 U.S.-based employees, is doling out historically high pay increases, cutting healthcare premiums by 10% and expanding benefits, including twice-annual companywide breaks that give workers at least nine consecutive days off.\par \par \uc2 Given current CEO optimism Mr. Knopp predicts the job market will stay tight for several years, and perhaps longer.\par \par \uc2 A recent \uc2 Conference Board\uc2  report found that companies are reserving an average of 3.9% of total payroll for wage increases this year, the largest jump since 2008. Nearly 40% of respondents said higher inflation factored into their decision.\par \par \uc2 Beyond pay, more companies are also soliciting feedback and seeking ways to improve worker retention, including more career development and an emphasis on culture, says Melissa Swift, U.S. transformation leader at consulting firm \uc2 Mercer LLC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For workers like Matt Connors of Pawtucket, R.I., a flexible work culture has become nonnegotiable. Mr. Connors, age 33, recently quit his software-engineering job after he and colleagues were required to go back to the office; they were frustrated to be called back after so many months of proving how productive they could be while working from home. "The writing was on the wall," he says. He promptly found a new job that pays 10% more and allows him to work remotely on a permanent basis.\par \par \uc2 Amid political upheaval and the pandemic, trust in institutions has lately fallen, according to the annual Trust Barometer survey of more than 36,000 people polled across 28 countries by public-relations firm Edelman. Yet workers demonstrate optimism in collective action.\par \par \uc2 Among 15,000 employees that Edelman surveyed about worker-employer trust, 69% said they want their employer to reflect their values and their CEO to be willing to take strong stances on social issues they support. And 56% believe a large group of employees exerting strong pressure from within can get an organization to change almost anything about itself.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. has seen an uptick in worker activism in the past year, as many employees pushed for action on racial equity following George Floyd's murder, and union drives and strikes hit companies from \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "That's putting heat on," says \uc2 Richard Edelman\uc2 , the company's CEO. "We thought that was simply a Gen Z phenomenon. It's not."\par \par \uc2 While many workers are switching companies, others are striking out on their own, knowing they won't have trouble finding work if a solo venture fails. From January to October of last year, entrepreneurs applied to register federal tax-identification numbers for about 4.5 million new businesses, up 56% from the same period in 2019 and the highest number on record dating back to 2004.\par \par \uc2 Such was the case with Danielle McGregor, the wife of the Tampa-based IT professional who quit his job for a 30% raise. Ms. McGregor quit her nursing job during the pandemic and now runs an \uc2 Etsy\uc2  business selling digital planners. These days, she says she makes an average of $4,500 a month, which is more than she did as a nurse. Their family can afford more vacations and meals out.\par \par \uc2 "We're a lot happier," she says, of her and her husband's decisions to quit.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Chen is a Wall Street Journal reporter in Philadelphia. She can be reached at te-ping.chen@wsj.com. Kathryn Dill, a Journal reporter in New York, contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 4.5 million U.S. workers quit their jobs in November, a record 3% rate\par \par \uc2 69% of employees in a global survey said they want their employer to reflect their values; the same share said they want their CEO to address controversial issues they care about\par \par \uc2 56% said a large group of employees exerting pressure from within can get an organization to change almost anything about itself\par \par \uc2 Sources: Labor Department (quits); Edelman Trust\par \par \uc2 Barometer 2022 survey of about 15,000 employees from around the world, Nov. 2021\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220118ei1i00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
