\par\par \b \uc2 Kroger Workers Walk Out In Denver\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jaewon Kang \par \uc2 683 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of workers at supermarket operator Kroger Co. went on strike in Denver, the latest push by employees who said they are seeking higher wages, more benefits and safer workplaces.\par \par \uc2 About 8,400 unionized workers at \uc2 Kroger\uc2 's King Soopers stores in Denver walked off the job at 7 a.m. ET on Wednesday, according to \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and United Food and Commercial Workers Local 7, which represents King Soopers workers in Colorado. The strike currently is affecting 77 of 151 King Soopers locations, a company spokesperson said after it began.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  said Wednesday that its stores would remain open and that it was "reckless and self-serving" for the union to strike. The supermarket operator said that it had hired temporary workers to help staff stores and that employees from other parts of the country had traveled to fill in.\par \par \uc2 Union officials representing \uc2 Kroger\uc2  workers declined to comment on Wednesday. They have previously said they want to secure a new contract with better wages, health and retirement benefits, and to ensure a safer work environment for employees when \uc2 Kroger\uc2  is generating higher profits.\par \par \uc2 The union officials said Tuesday that going on strike was the only way to get fairer terms after the company had offered unsatisfactory proposals throughout the past few months of negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  has said it remains committed to settling an agreement that will benefit workers and keep prices down for consumers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Kroger\uc2  strike comes as more workers demand better wages and benefits, seeking greater leverage as labor shortages challenge industries across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 About 1,400 \uc2 Kellogg Co\uc2 . workers recently ended a 2<frac12>-month strike by ratifying a new five-year contract. Workers at snack producer Mondelez International Inc. and farm-equipment maker Deere & Co. have also gone on strike in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 . baristas in Buffalo, N.Y., voted in December to form the first labor union in the coffee giant's 50-year history, while \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 .'s warehouse workers in Alabama are holding a second union election after workers there voted against unionizing in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Many U.S. grocers increased hourly wages for workers at the start of the pandemic but phased out so-called hazard pay months later. The labor market has since tightened, companies say, as unemployment checks and federal stimulus checks made it harder for them to find workers.\par \par \uc2 Retailers have offered overtime pay for workers who cover shifts, sick pay for employees who need to quarantine, and raised pay or provided referral bonuses to appeal to potential hires.\par \par \uc2 Workers and advocates have said supermarket employees deserve higher wages and more benefits because they are managing heavier workloads and stress while risking their health during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Some workers have also been tasked with managing anxious shoppers, and explaining or enforcing their employers' mask policies.\par \par \uc2 Tension between \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and the union representing its Colorado employees has been escalating in recent weeks as the two sides tried to negotiate a new collective-bargaining agreement.\par \par \uc2 The union in December sued King Soopers, accusing the \uc2 Kroger\uc2 -owned supermarket chain of breaching the collective-bargaining agreement by using third-party staffing services to hire workers in stores and using these workers to perform roles covered by the union.\par \par \uc2 During negotiations between the union and the company, King Soopers employees voted to strike on Jan. 2. The previous agreement expired at 11:59 p.m. on Jan. 8.\par \par \uc2 This week, King Soopers filed unfair labor practices charges against the union for refusing to bargain in good faith. The company said union leaders refused repeated requests to return to the negotiating table and rejected mediation services, adding that the union had given no indication of when it would re-engage in discussions.\par \par \uc2 The company said Tuesday that it offered its "last, best and final" offer, representing an investment of $170 million over the next three years in higher wages and expanded healthcare benefits.\par \par \uc2 The union declined King Soopers' offer Tuesday, saying that the company didn't respond to its requests on wage, health and safety matters.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220113ei1d0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
