\par\par \b \uc2 Meta Sets Booster Requirement\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter \par \uc2 645 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meta Platforms Inc\uc2  . said that it would require Covid-19 booster shots for employees to work from its U.S. campuses and that it would delay fully opening those offices until late March, in another sign that the Omicron variant is shifting corporate reopening plans.\par \par \uc2 The technology company, which owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and WhatsApp, said Monday that, beginning March 28, employees who meet eligibility guidelines to receive the shots would need to show proof of a booster vaccination to use its offices.\par \par \uc2 "Boosters provide increased protection," a Meta spokesman said. "Given the evidence of booster effectiveness, we are expanding our vaccination requirement to include boosters."\par \par \uc2 More companies are now putting such requirements in place, or offering incentives for workers to get additional doses.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America Corp\uc2  . said last week it would donate $100 to a food bank or hunger-relief organization for every U.S. employee who shows proof of a booster through the end of January. Executives at \uc2 Jefferies Financial Group Inc\uc2  . told staff in early December that the company would require everyone entering its offices or attending a company event to have a booster administered by Jan. 31, unless they are ineligible to receive one.\par \par \uc2 Industrial real-estate company \uc2 Prologis Inc\uc2  . recently began requiring employees in the U.S. to get the Covid-19 booster within 30 days of their eligibility to receive the shot, unless they have a medical or religious exemption. Other companies, including meatpacker \uc2 JBS USA Holdings Inc\uc2  . and Benchmark Senior Living LLC, an operator of assisted-living facilities, have hosted booster clinics at company sites to make it easier for employees to get another shot.\par \par \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  hasn't changed the definition of what it means to be "fully vaccinated," but in a press briefing last week, CDC Director Dr. \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2  said the agency is now recommending that individuals stay "up to date" once they become eligible for additional doses. For policy makers, changing the definition of what it means to be fully vaccinated to also include boosters would be complicated because it would affect vaccine mandates that are being challenged in courts, The Wall Street Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 New York Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  said last week that healthcare workers in the state would be required to get boosters to better protect them against Covid-19. California Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  also said last month that state healthcare workers and employees in high-risk, congregate settings would be required to get a booster by Feb. 1.\par \par \uc2 Meta had planned for its U.S. offices to fully reopen in late January. The company previously told the most of its U.S. staff that they can do their jobs remotely, if desired, or temporarily push back a return by an additional three to five months.\par \par \uc2 The latest delay means a number of employees at Meta, and other white-collar companies, will have worked remotely for about two years since the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  's 2020 pandemic declaration. Many companies had hoped to bring workers back in January, but the rapid rise in Covid-19 cases over the past few weeks has led many employers to again close offices, or to indefinitely pause reopening plans. Executives across the corporate landscape have said that it will be increasingly difficult to bring people back to offices after employees have grown so accustomed to doing their jobs from their homes.\par \par \uc2 "We understand that the continued uncertainty makes this a difficult time to make decisions about where to work, so we're giving more time to choose what works best for them," said Janelle Gale, a vice president of human resources at Meta.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  has encouraged employees at its U.S. offices to work remotely in recent weeks, amid rising Covid-19 infection rates nationally.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 David Benoit contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220111ei1b0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
