\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Chicago Teacher Walkout Ends With Virus Pact\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Barrett \par \uc2 268 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO -- Officials announced a resolution to a teacher walkout over Covid-19 protocols as the city hit records for new cases of the virus.\par \par \uc2 Teachers will return to schools on Tuesday, a week after they voted to switch to online-only teaching, and students will begin classes again on Wednesday, said Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jesse Sharkey\uc2 , president of the Chicago Teachers Union, said the union's governing body on Monday voted 63% to 37% to end the walkout. He described months of talks to get the city and the school system to agree to a system that would protect teachers, students and parents from the virus. "We're going to keep doing what's right for this city," he said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lightfoot said the key to the agreement was setting a metric for how many Covid-19 infections would be needed to trigger a shutdown of individual schools. "I'm glad that we're putting this behind us and looking forward. People are frustrated. Why wouldn't they be?" Ms. Lightfoot said.\par \par \uc2 School administrators and the union in the nation's third-largest school system have clashed repeatedly over Covid-19 protocols. Classes were online for much of the 2020-21 school year and began returning to in-person instruction last February amid strong objections from the union.\par \par \uc2 Classes have been in person this school year, though the union and the schools had never adopted a formal agreement on safety protocols. The city has been averaging about 5,000 new Covid-19 cases a day.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220111ei1b00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
