\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Vaccine Rule Takes Effect --- Mandate for large employers starts, even as high court weighs legal challenge\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Harrison \par \uc2 586 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2022\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Much of the Biden administration's vaccination mandate for larger employers went into effect Monday despite the possibility the Supreme Court could halt the rule as it considers a legal challenge to it.\par \par \uc2 As of Jan. 10, all employers with more than 100 employees must have a procedure in place to ensure employees are vaccinated and keep track of workers' vaccination status. Employers must also track whether their workers are infected and keep those who test positive away from work. Workers who aren't vaccinated must wear a mask while indoors.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department in December gave employers an extra month to require that unvaccinated employees take weekly Covid-19 tests. It goes into effect Feb. 9.\par \par \uc2 The rule covers roughly 84 million workers. It doesn't apply to businesses where employees don't report to a central workplace. It also doesn't apply to those whose employees work at home or outdoors.\par \par \uc2 Hanging over the vaccine mandate is the prospect that the Supreme Court could block its implementation at any time while it considers a challenge from business groups.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the Supreme Court heard oral arguments with a majority of justices questioning the administration's authority to issue such a mandate, a sign the court could strike down the mandate. Justices also suggested they could put the rule on hold while they deliberate.\par \par \uc2 "Every minute of uncertainty is adding to the problems small-business owners are already facing," said Karen Harned, executive director of the \uc2 National Federation of Independent Businesses\uc2 ' legal center.\par \par \uc2 The NFIB is one of the groups challenging the rule. Businesses are divided over the mandate.\par \par \uc2 If the rule survives the legal challenge, workers not vaccinated as of Feb. 9 would have to submit to weekly testing, according to a document posted on the Labor Department's website. Employees are considered fully vaccinated if they have taken both doses of a two-dose vaccine. In other words, they would be considered vaccinated even if they haven't received a booster shot, according to the agency.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Labor Department's Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  "will work closely with the regulated community to provide compliance assistance," a department spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 Many employers, however, prefer to wait for the court's decision before setting up a vaccine mandate, said \uc2 Emily Dickens\uc2  , head of government affairs at the \uc2 Society for Human Resource Management\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 About 51% of employers subject to the rule surveyed by the society last month said they wouldn't put a vaccination policy in place until the legal process is complete, she said. And about 18% said they wouldn't comply with the requirement even if the mandate is upheld.\par \par \uc2 "They're taking the risk to see what's happening with the court," Ms. Dickens said.\par \par \uc2 Employers worry the vaccine mandate will have a bad effect on morale and will lead workers to look for other jobs at companies not covered by the mandate, she said. Employers also are concerned about the added paperwork burden, she said.\par \par \uc2 The rule is being issued as an emergency temporary standard, which means it can stay in place for six months. After that, it must go through a lengthier rule-making process before coming into effect.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is also considering a challenge to a separate rule from the Biden administration, requiring that staff at healthcare facilities receiving Medicaid and Medicare funding receive vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020220111ei1b0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
