\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 CIOs Seek Level Playing Field For At-Home, On-Site Staff\b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabelle Bousquette \par \uc2 665 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 December 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Chief information officers, who helped enable the shift to remote work in the early days of the Covid-19 pandemic, are now working to revamp office space and processes to make the workplace more inclusive for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 Hybrid and remote work became more firmly entrenched in 2021 as the Delta and Omicron variants delayed plans for a broad return to the office, prompting executives to take steps to make sure the system is working for everyone.\par \par \uc2 "That integration of the physical world and the virtual world has probably been the most complicated," said Yael Cosset, CIO of \uc2 Kroger Co\uc2 ., describing his efforts to create the hybrid office.\par \par \uc2 Part of the problem, he said, is ensuring a "level playing field" for those who are in the office and those who are at home. Cincinnati-based \uc2 Kroger\uc2  , the largest U.S. supermarket chain by revenue, has installed camera and audio hardware in its conference rooms, although for larger conference rooms, it is still difficult to find the right audio-balancing technology that picks up speaker's voices but not background noise, Mr. Cosset said.\par \par \uc2 Paul von Autenried\uc2 , CIO of \uc2 Bristol-Myers Squibb Co\uc2 ., said the New York-based biopharmaceutical company is operating under a hybrid model. During meetings, employees in conference rooms generally join from their laptops and turn their cameras on so virtual participants can see everyone's face, he said.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, he said, "We're generally finding the practice that people are still raising their hands when they want to speak, and by doing that, it also encourages a greater degree of inclusivity . . . so that the remote workers are not disadvantaged in those moments of wanting to interject."\par \par \uc2 Anil Bhatt, CIO of health insurer \uc2 Anthem Inc\uc2 ., is also working to bridge the divide between remote and on-site employees. The Indianapolis-based company has installed tablets at seats in conference rooms. The tablets' cameras show the face of each person in the conference room, rather than one wide shot of the whole group. The benefit is that employees working remotely can still see the faces of those in the conference room, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Conference rooms and the conference-room technologies were designed with remote work as an afterthought," said Edward Wagoner, CIO, digital at Chicago-based commercial real-estate services company \uc2 Jones Lang LaSalle Inc\uc2  . "We need to redesign them to be more inclusive," he said, adding that the reason people will be coming into offices now will be "to collaborate and socialize," not just to sit and answer emails.\par \par \uc2 Beyond conference rooms, some CIOs are also looking to optimize whole offices.\par \par \uc2 Healthcare company \uc2 Centene Corp\uc2  . is building an East Coast campus in Charlotte, N.C., to complement its St. Louis headquarters and its West Coast campus in Sacramento, Calif. The first phase of construction is expected to be completed in 2022, good timing for the company to optimize hybrid work in the campus's design, said CIO \uc2 Mark Brooks\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The new campus will have "fusion centers" designed for collaboration, Mr. Brooks said. One room will have a video wall in the middle that can serve as a partition if teams need to work independently, or it can be used to let the teams present content on both sides of the wall, he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cosset said \uc2 Kroger\uc2  is leveraging data about employees' work habits to provide them with personalized tips about how they can work more efficiently, whether from home or the office. The tips, which come in the form of a weekly email, can tell employees things like how often they are getting distracted by emails when they are in a meeting or how long they have been working without taking a break.\par \par \uc2 Employees can opt in if they want to receive such tips, he said. "We wanted to really make sure we were not turning into too much prescription at the individual level," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211224ehco0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
