\par\par \uc2 Heard on the Street\par \b \uc2 Big Videogames Make Costly Misfires\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dan Gallagher \par \uc2 592 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 December 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 Even big, experienced videogame makers get it wrong sometimes. Having all do so at the same time is rare.\par \par \uc2 In an unusual twist to the holiday selling season, the biggest games from the three biggest game publishers have made notable misfires. \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2 's "Call of Duty: \uc2 Vanguard\uc2 " has garnered the lowest critic scores of the franchise and has reportedly recorded weak initial sales. "Battlefield 2042" from \uc2 Electronic Arts\uc2  also has fared poorly with critics and suffered from some technical issues during its launch. Bugs also hurt the launch of "Grand Theft Auto: The Trilogy" from \uc2 Take-Two Interactive Software\uc2 , with that game also getting savaged by reviewers -- a rarity for the popular franchise. It was named the sixth-worst game of the year by the critic score amalgamation site Metacritic.\par \par \uc2 All three games went on sale in November. They face strong competition from online free-to-play titles such as Epic Games' "Fortnite." "Halo Infinite" launched by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  on Dec. 8 also has turned out to be surprisingly strong, following a major delay last year.\par \par \uc2 EA, Take-Two and \uc2 Activision\uc2  have yet to report any financial results from their recent launches. \uc2 Activision\uc2  has typically issued some sort of update following its annual "Call of Duty" installment, but the company is now beset with an internal crisis stemming from its alleged treatment of female workers.\par \par \uc2 EA and Take-Two aren't facing the same predicament. But both have been affected by the pandemic's impact on game production, with so many developers still working remotely. At an investment conference on the day of the "Grand Theft Auto" release, Take-Two Chief Executive \uc2 Strauss Zelnick\uc2  said "I don't think work-from-home is as productive," though he also noted that new technology used in the most cutting-edge games also has an impact on development times. EA had already delayed the release of "Battlefield 2042" by a month to its Nov. 19 launch date, citing the work-from-home challenge.\par \par \uc2 Investors already were tepid on the videogame sector, given concerns about the industry's difficult comparisons to last year's pandemic-fueled surge in game play. Analysts expect combined net bookings for \uc2 Activision\uc2 , EA and Take-Two to rise 10% this calendar year following a 23% jump last year, according to FactSet.\par \par \uc2 EA's and Take-Two's stocks were down 4% and 12% for the year preceding their respective game launches, compared with the S&P 500's 25% gain by mid-November. Both stocks have given more ground since, though nowhere near the selloff that has taken nearly one-third of \uc2 Activision\uc2 's market value since the state of California sued the company over its workplace environment in July.\par \par \uc2 Activision\uc2  will likely remain under that cloud as its internal crisis creates additional uncertainty for its future game pipeline. But EA and Take-Two can both manage through their respective misfires. EA's online, free-to-play "Apex Legends" is competing well in the shooter category; Corey Barrett of MScience noted that the game's bookings growth "materially reaccelerated in November" in a recent report.\par \par \uc2 Take-Two, meanwhile, will soon start showing the results of a major buildup of its game pipeline. Analysts expect double-digit growth in bookings for the company's next two fiscal years compared with a projected 2% drop for the current one ending in March. Given their currently depressed valuations, EA and Take-Two are in a good position to level up.\par \par \uc2 page,5043 \par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211224ehco0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
