\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  of the Ivy League\b0\par\par\uc2 747 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 December 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 What do \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 . and \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  have in common? Their workers are represented by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  and have gone on strike this fall seeking higher compensation. Yet the contrasts are instructive about the state of higher education.\par \par \uc2 Many Columbia class sections have been canceled since early November, and final grades have been thrown in jeopardy due to a seven-week strike by the Student Workers of Columbia-UAW, which includes 3,000 graduates and undergraduates who assist with teaching, grading and tutoring. Columbia recently told undergrads they could choose to receive a pass-fail in any course this semester in "appreciation of how difficult this term may have been for you." Many would prefer actual grades.\par \par \uc2 While workers at companies like \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and Amazon have rejected unions, Big Labor is winning with university employees. Last week the \uc2 UAW\uc2  was recognized as labor representative for 17,000 student researchers at the University of California. About a quarter (100,000) of \uc2 UAW\uc2  members are university employees.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 It's worth reflecting on why unions are having more success on campus. No doubt academic workers are liberal. But it's also true that they are often treated poorly by universities to support high-earning administrators and tenured faculty.\par \par \uc2 Consider Columbia, where many student workers earn little more than New York City's $15 hourly minimum wage. Columbia this spring offered to raise its minimum hourly wage to $17, and the salary of doctoral candidates with 12-month teaching appointments to $42,350. Students rejected the proposal. Columbia's latest offer is $20 an hour and $43,621 for full-year positions.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  wants $45,000 for 12-month appointments and a minimum $26 hourly rate for non-salaried workers, with annual increases. The union says a single adult had to make at least $45,285 to live in Manhattan in June 2020 -- before this year's inflation.\par \par \uc2 "Columbia made $3.1 billion in returns on its investments this past year alone," the union says, adding that its demands for a three-year contract would amount to a mere "3% of its increase in net assets from investments." Columbia charges $63,530 in undergraduate tuition and fees ($82,584 including room and board), which over four years is as much as a cost of a house. President \uc2 Lee Bollinger\uc2  earns roughly $4.6 million, and full-time professors make on average $268,400.\par \par \uc2 The university has nonetheless held firm and last week warned that striking workers might not be offered positions this spring. The union has accused the university of retaliation and filed unfair labor practice charges with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . Columbia says it is offering PhD students "one of the most generous packages of any university in the country."\par \par \uc2 We take no side in this dispute, though you have to smile at what the Marxists might call the contradictions of academic production. When \uc2 Kellogg Co\uc2 . last week threatened to replace striking workers, President Biden called it "an existential attack on the union and its members' jobs and livelihoods." Deere and Kellogg pay their factory workers more than Columbia does its academic grunts. Kellogg says most workers at its cereal factories earned $120,000 last year.\par \par \uc2 Democrats seem less perturbed by the Ivy League school's hardball bargaining. Seven New York Democrats sent a letter last week to Mr. Bollinger that couldn't have been more gentle, encouraging the university "to strengthen its efforts in good faith bargaining."\par \par \uc2 Columbia's faculty have also professed solidarity with student workers, which costs nothing. But they haven't stepped in to help deliver the education for which undergrads are paying a small fortune. Deere and Kellogg used salaried workers to keep their plants running during union strikes. Is it too much to ask tenured professors to grade exams?\par \par \uc2 Companies that underpay workers and mistreat customers won't survive long. But universities with brand names have a captive clientele as well as steady subsidies in the form of federal student loans. This is why many were able to get away with keeping classes remote last year without discounting tuition.\par \par \uc2 The next time you hear professors lecture about inequality in American, ask them about the state of the academic working class.\par \par \uc2 (See related letters: "Letters to the Editor: Columbia Replies: Ph.D.s Are Treated Fairly" -- WSJ Dec. 27, 2021)\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: How Generous Is \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  Really?" -- WSJ Jan. 3, 2022)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211218ehci0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
