\par\par \b \uc2 Tight Labor Market Sends Jobless Claims to New Low\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Harrison \par \uc2 841 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A strong economic recovery and persistent labor shortage pushed jobless claims to their lowest level in more than half a century last week, just 18 months after the pandemic prompted six million workers to file for unemployment in one week.\par \par \uc2 First-time claims for unemployment benefits, a proxy for layoffs, fell to 184,000 in the week ended Dec. 4, the lowest level since September 1969, the Labor Department said Thursday. The previous pre-pandemic low of 194,000 was recorded late last month.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment claims have been steadily falling all year as the labor market has tightened amid a shortage of available workers. While the Omicron variant of Covid-19 represents another threat from the pandemic, economists expect labor market conditions to remain tight.\par \par \uc2 Claims have now fallen below where they were in the year before the pandemic, when they averaged 218,000.\par \par \uc2 The steady decline in filings is an indication that employers are reluctant to lay off workers as jobs are plentiful, consumer demand is high and the pool of prospective workers remains lower than before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The labor market is unusually tight, with workers in high demand and jobs going unfilled. In October, there were two unemployed workers for every three job openings.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate last month fell to 4.2% from 4.6% in October, the Labor Department said last week, but hiring slowed. While almost 600,000 joined the workforce, there are still 2.4 million fewer people in the labor force than before the pandemic hit.\par \par \uc2 The tight labor market is expected to keep unemployment filings close to their pre-pandemic level, said \uc2 Nancy Vanden Houten\uc2 , lead economist at Oxford Economics.\par \par \uc2 "We expect claims will start to more consistently hover around pre-Covid averages of 220,000 or perhaps slightly lower given current tight labor market conditions," she said.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment claims rose sharply in March 2020, as the first few days of pandemic-related lockdowns caused employers to lay off workers in droves. New filings hit a record 6.1 million in April 2020, eclipsing the previous record of 695,000 in 1982.\par \par \uc2 Multiple rounds of stimulus legislation extended the length of time workers could receive benefits for up to 18 months and offered an extra $300 a week. The enhanced payments expired nationwide in September as the economy recovered.\par \par \uc2 More unemployed workers should eventually get new jobs as they exhaust their benefits, said \uc2 Ms. Vanden Houten\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This assumes the Omicron variant of the coronavirus doesn't have a major negative impact on the economy," she said, referring to the latest variant of Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Economists say the low number partly reflects seasonal adjustments that have been thrown off balance by the pandemic's disruptions, but that the broader downward trend is firmly in place.\par \par \uc2 Recent warm weather in parts of the country could also have delayed the seasonal layoffs of construction workers, which pushed down the overall number last week, said \uc2 Joshua Shapiro\uc2 , chief U.S. economist at MFR Inc. Once the weather turns cold and those workers stop working, the layoff numbers should climb slightly, he said.\par \par \uc2 "This whole seasonal adjustment issue combined with the fact that nobody wants to fire anybody contributes to the very low number we're seeing," he said.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, there are fewer people in the workforce, making it hard for employers to fill open positions.\par \par \uc2 For instance, \uc2 United Natural Foods Inc\uc2 ., a bulk food provider, has raised wages and has started offering flexible schedules and earlier paychecks as a way to retain and attract new employees, said \uc2 Christopher Testa\uc2 , the company's president, in an earnings call on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 He said he expects the changes "to contribute to long-lasting associate retainment and consistent performance for our customers."\par \par \uc2 Roughly 82.1% of people ages 25 to 54 years old are either working or looking for work, the Labor Department said. That is a lower level than in February 2020, when it stood at 83.1%.\par \par \uc2 In October, there were about 3.6 million more job openings than unemployed workers, the Labor Department said. That has prompted employers to look for ways to compete for new workers.\par \par \uc2 Private-sector wages rose 4.8% last month from the previous year.\par \par \uc2 Katherine Dobbs is one such worker who has benefited from rising wages. After contracting Covid-19 last year, Ms. Dobbs was out for three months from her job as an emergency dispatcher in Florida, much of that time without pay.\par \par \uc2 Once she recovered, she moved to Grayson, Ga., to be closer to family and enrolled in a phlebotomy program. In March, she started working as a medical screener for an orthopedic practice making $13 an hour. In the eight months since, she has been promoted twice, most recently in November. She now makes $16 an hour.\par \par \uc2 "When I got the job it was pretty entry-level," said Ms. Dobbs. "Just to so quickly start moving up was really cool and kind of unexpected."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich406\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6090
