\par\par \b \uc2 Cybersecurity (A Special Report) --- The Problem With Shaming Employees: It will only make you more vulnerable to hacking\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Renaud \par \uc2 607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cybercriminals send many emails to an organization's employees, hoping to deceive them into clicking on a link or opening an attachment. Sometimes, an employee will do just that.\par \par \uc2 It's what the organization does next that is so crucial.\par \par \uc2 Organizations often respond to this kind of incident by blaming and shaming the employee who triggered the incident, no matter how honest or understandable the mistake. The assumption is that the employee will exercise more care in the future to avoid a repeat experience. After all, who would want to be called out again?\par \par \uc2 But their assumption is wrong.\par \par \uc2 When someone falls prey to a cyberattack, they can experience one of two emotions: shame or guilt. While both are self-conscious emotions, they are very different. People who are shamed feel rejected and often respond defensively by withdrawing or getting angry; they feel there is no road to redemption for them. Those who experience guilt are able to accept responsibility for the mistake, without feeling rejected.\par \par \uc2 That's because if somebody feels shame, the focus is on them, not the mistake. If somebody feels guilt, the focus is on the mistake.\par \par \uc2 The most concerning consequence of shame is that the shamed are more likely to feel less loyalty to their organizations, and engage in unethical behaviors. In the cybersecurity context, this has profound implications. When people no longer feel loyal, why would they care enough to behave securely?\par \par \uc2 To better understand the aftermath of such cybersecurity incidents, Rosalind Searle from the University of Glasgow, Marc Dupuis from the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2  and I asked survey respondents whether they had caused a cybersecurity incident at work. If they had, they reported that they immediately felt bad. But how they felt next depended on what their employer did.\par \par \uc2 Respondents fell into two distinct groups. In the first group, people talked about managers yelling at them, embarrassing them in front of their peers and not trusting them after the incident.\par \par \uc2 It was clear from their comments that these employees felt shame and rejection, and that the employer-employee relationship was damaged, perhaps irretrievably.\par \par \uc2 Those in the second group said that their mistake had been met with understanding and support. They were told how to repair the situation. The consequence, in contrast to the other group, was a much stronger relationship between the employer and employee after the incident, and a desire to do better in the future.\par \par \uc2 What does this mean for organizations? The destructiveness of shame, when used as a behavioral-modification tool to bring employees into line, leads to a situation where no one wins. This doesn't mean that employees aren't held accountable for their mistakes. What it does mean is that the focus should be on helping the person to correct their mistake and do better in the future.\par \par \uc2 The implications of our survey were clear: Shame is similar to a boomerang that will come back to hurt the organization, as well as harming the employee. Managers should deal with the mistake, but not reject the employee. If employees feel that their personhood is being attacked, they will respond defensively. Shaming results in a lose-lose outcome.\par \par \uc2 Employees can be an organization's greatest asset when it comes to defeating the efforts of cybercriminals. Using shame as a behavior modification tool squanders that potential. And that's the real shame.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Dr. Renaud is a chancellor's fellow at the \uc2 University of Strathclyde\uc2  in Glasgow, Scotland. She can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211208ehc800003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
