\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 's Parting Insult\b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 December 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  has done enormous damage to New York City over his eight years in office, and most New Yorkers will be elated to see him leave on Dec. 31. True to form, however, on Monday he announced one more progressive parting gift: A coercive and counterproductive Covid vaccine mandate.\par \par \uc2 The mayor is requiring that all private workers in the city be vaccinated by Dec. 27, which he called "a pre-emptive strike" against a virus surge this winter. The mandate is a strike against the city, not the virus. It will yield diminishing public-health benefits while making it harder for the city to recover economically from his and former New York Gov. Andrew Cuomo's destructive lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2  has already required vaccines for city government workers, teachers and anyone who wants to eat at a restaurant, use a gym or see a show in the city. This has harmed small businesses and tourism. Yet apparently these mandates aren't coercive enough for Hizzoner. So now he's giving the unvaccinated an ultimatum: Get one shot by Dec. 27, leave the city or stay unemployed.\par \par \uc2 Way to kick businesses when they're down. New York City had a 9.4% unemployment rate in October, but businesses say they still can't find enough workers, especially in the hospitality trade. The mandate will make it even harder since most of the city's unvaccinated are lower income.\par \par \uc2 State data show that 95% of adults in Manhattan, 87% in the Bronx and 84% in Brooklyn have received at least one dose. Many more have natural immunity from prior infection.\par \par \uc2 Hospitalizations are increasing mostly upstate where vaccination rates are lower and fewer people have natural immunity. While vaccines are protective against severe illness, they don't prevent infection or transmission. Unvaccinated workers are mainly a threat to themselves. \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2 's mandate threatens small businesses struggling under the city's tax burden and expensive labor regulations.\par \par \uc2 Kathryn Wylde\uc2 , CEO of the Partnership for New York City, said businesses "were blindsided" by the mayor's announcement, adding "there's no forewarning, no discussion, no idea about whether it's legal or who he expects to enforce it."\par \par \uc2 The mandate -- the first nationwide, as far as we know -- is a nasty and needless infringement on liberty. There's also this contradiction: \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2  wants to punish law-abiding workers who have done nothing wrong, while his anti-police policies have encouraged a surge of violent crime and made major public areas of the city dangerous again.\par \par \uc2 Guidance on how businesses are supposed to enforce the mandate and penalties will supposedly come on Dec. 15. It will take effect on Dec. 27, four days before mayor-elect Eric Adams takes office and we hope scraps the mandate. The city can't be rid of \uc2 Mr. de Blasio\uc2  soon enough.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211207ehc70000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
