\par\par \b \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Gets Federal Order To Hold New Union Vote\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sebastian Herrera \par \uc2 1,042 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal official ordered a new unionization vote for \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . workers in Alabama, saying the company violated labor law in its campaign against organizing earlier this year, according to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 About 71% of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees who voted in Bessemer, Ala., rejected unionization in April, with many saying they were wary of the cost of union dues and weren't persuaded that a union would be able to boost their pay or benefits.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  ruled that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's actions throughout the mail-in election -- including its decision to install a \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  collection box that could have confused employees -- were grounds to nullify the results and hold the vote again. It is unclear when a new vote will be held, and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  might yet appeal to the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 's governing board in Washington, according to a spokeswoman for the agency.\par \par \uc2 "Today's decision confirms what we were saying all along -- that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's intimidation and interference prevented workers from having a fair say in whether they wanted a union in their workplace," said \uc2 Stuart Appelbaum\uc2 , president of the Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union. The RWDSU ran the campaign to unionize \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 An Amazon spokeswoman said company employees "have always had the choice of whether or not to join a union, and they overwhelmingly chose not to join the RWDSU earlier this year. It's disappointing that the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has now decided that those votes shouldn't count." The spokeswoman said a union would hamper the efficiency of improving company policies quickly and nimbly. The company has previously said that it didn't violate labor laws and treats workers fairly.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said that the election results are evidence of its standing with workers. After the vote concluded, Chairman \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  said that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  needed a "better vision" for its workforce. He set a company goal to be "Earth's best employer" and "Earth's safest place to work."\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  can appeal the decision to the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 's governing board in Washington, according to the NLRB spokeswoman. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  is a federal government agency in charge of enforcing U.S. labor law in collective bargaining and worker disputes.\par \par \uc2 With Democrats holding a majority of the five-person board, labor researchers expect the board to side with workers more than during former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's administration.\par \par \uc2 Another election could happen sometime in the next several months, and the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  may schedule a vote that is held before its governing board rules on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's appeal, according to an agency spokeswoman.\par \par \uc2 Working conditions for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse employees have been in focus since the pandemic forced widespread lockdowns of schools and offices, prompting an uptick in online shopping that boosted demand for the company's products.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in several facilities walked out last year over working conditions. The company has strict performance rates and break policies that have drawn ire from labor activists and some government officials. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has also been found to record higher injury rates than the national average, according to federal workplace data. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has disputed those figures, saying it reports injuries more comprehensively than other companies.\par \par \uc2 Employee activism is on the rise at many of the world's largest technology companies, where workers have objected to business dealings with the U.S. armed services, climate-mitigation policies they deemed inferior, mandates to return to work or broad treatment of certain classes of workers.\par \par \uc2 Some employees at \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 .'s \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . and other companies have taken issue with management and taken their complaints public. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , however, is unique: A majority of its employees work in warehouses, are relatively unskilled and are on the front lines of a battle for low-skilled workers across the country.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Henderson, the regional director overseeing the case for the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , ruled that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's actions throughout the mail-in election were grounds to nullify the results and have the vote be held again.\par \par \uc2 In her ruling, Ms. Henderson said she agreed with an \uc2 NLRB\uc2  hearing officer in early August who found that the mailbox \uc2 Amazon\uc2  set up "interfered with the laboratory conditions necessary to conduct a fair election." That is because it could have made employees believe \uc2 Amazon\uc2  played a role in collecting and counting ballots, she said. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  wasn't found to have accessed any ballots submitted through the mailbox.\par \par \uc2 In addition, employees could have perceived the company was tracking their vote through "vote no" pins and tags the company handed out because \uc2 Amazon\uc2  managers could see which employees took the pins, the hearing officer said.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has said the collection box was installed as a convenience to employees, and that it didn't seek to intimidate workers throughout the vote.\par \par \uc2 The Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union would face an uphill battle on any new vote, given the margin of victory and employee turnover at the facility, labor researchers said.\par \par \uc2 Alabama workers began their campaign about a year ago. Organizers said a union would give workers more leverage in any disputes with the company and allow them to collectively bargain over safety standards, training, breaks, pay and other benefits, complaints that have popped up across many company facilities. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  held regular meetings inside the Alabama facility to make its case that unionization was unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 While some employees said they believed a union could help them negotiate better terms with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  on issues such as breaks, other workers said they were wary of union dues and weren't persuaded that the union would be able to add significantly to their pay or improve benefits.\par \par \uc2 The company has repeatedly raised wages this year to compete for workers as a labor shortage has rippled through the U.S. economy. In some areas, new \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees have received hiring bonuses and pay around $20 an hour, and the company's starting pay now averages more than $18 an hour. In 2018, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  made its starting wage $15 an hour. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  doesn't have any unionized employees in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 A challenge for organizers is \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's high rate of turnover, with facilities across the company's network regularly churning through workers.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211130ehbu0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
