\par\par \b \uc2 Workplace Technology (A Special Report) --- Older Workers and Tech: Many seniors struggle with new technology. Let's help.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stefan Tams \par \uc2 965 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par R5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Constantly updated and reconfigured, office software is the bane of older workers -- whose struggles with IT lead to frustration and lower productivity.\par \par \uc2 But does it have to be that way?\par \par \uc2 At the business school HEC Montreal in Canada, I studied the performance of hundreds of older workers on job tasks that require the use of common organizational software such as spreadsheets. Here's the bad news: I consistently found that several of the software problems older workers face are caused by cognitive changes that come with age.\par \par \uc2 It takes them longer to process information. It is harder for them to ignore distractions such as system notifications. And they find it harder to figure out novel situations. In one study I conducted, older workers reported using, on average, just 0.5 of six new features in Excel 2019. Younger workers used an average of two of the new features regularly.\par \par \uc2 This isn't true, of course, for all employees. But it's true for enough of them that it can cause problems in an organization.\par \par \uc2 While managers can't stop cognitive decline, there are all sorts of things they can do to help older workers increase their productivity and experience less stress when using office technology.\par \par \uc2 Here's some advice for making technology less painful for older workers.\par \par \uc2 Train and train again\par \par \uc2 It seems like repeated training for older workers would be a no-brainer. Yet even though HR typically recommends such training, many managers don't follow through.\par \par \uc2 For one thing, it is expensive and time-consuming. And, managers figure, older workers close to retirement will use that training for a shorter time than younger ones.\par \par \uc2 It is a shortsighted attitude. Continual training improves memory and helps older workers process information faster. It also lets them use technology more comfortably -- all of which more than makes up for the cost.\par \par \uc2 Train face to face\par \par \uc2 It is important to do that training right, however. And one crucial part of that is training older workers face to face and not online.\par \par \uc2 Unlike younger workers, who are often more comfortable doing things remotely, older workers place greater value on in-person interactions. They are reassuring, they stimulate more positive emotions, and they require fewer cognitive resources.\par \par \uc2 Fix search engines\par \par \uc2 Not all of the changes fall on managers' shoulders. Software designers must develop software and websites that are friendly to older users.\par \par \uc2 One important change would be using search engines that make it easier to find information.\par \par \uc2 Think about how search engines typically work. You type in a phrase and get back an extensive list of results that you need to hunt through. The answer you want will usually be hidden among things you don't need.\par \par \uc2 Searching through all that can be taxing for older workers, who process information at lower speeds. For instance, in one study I conducted, workers must substitute a series of symbols for numbers based on a code they have been given. Older workers substituted 16 symbols for digits, on average, in 90 seconds, whereas younger ones substituted 21.\par \par \uc2 How to make search engines less taxing? There is an approach called "faceted" search, where you type in a phrase and instead of getting a list of links, the search engine first organizes the links into big, broad categories and lets you choose which one you want to see -- making things much easier to process. (You would also have the option to use a conventional search approach if you prefer.)\par \par \uc2 Let's say you type in a search term such as "account." You aren't shown any links yet. Instead, you see a list of categories related to the term -- perhaps assets, liabilities, equity, revenue and expenses.\par \par \uc2 If you then click on, say, "assets," you see the search results that concern assets -- but you also have the option to click on additional, narrower categories to focus your search even further. With "assets," you might have the option to drill down to search results for checking, petty cash or accounts receivable.\par \par \uc2 Along the way, there would be a clearly marked trail to show you all the clicks you have made, so if you want to backtrack, you won't get lost.\par \par \uc2 Less distracting distractions\par \par \uc2 It is also important to cut down on-screen interruptions. In an experiment I conducted with over 100 older and younger people, I discovered that older workers have a harder time ignoring distractions, such as meeting reminders, because they have impaired selective attention. If messages and alerts always appeared at the same location, older workers could predict where they would occur -- and make them less of an interruption.\par \par \uc2 Don't generalize\par \par \uc2 Talk of diminishing capacities and struggles with IT should not be a pretext for underestimating older workers. My research results are based on averages, and don't apply to all employees.\par \par \uc2 And almost all older workers have strengths that can help them compensate for cognitive decline. Abilities like information-processing speed decline with age, but ones like transferring knowledge from one application to another improve. Office 365 provides a good example. Since Word and Excel are structured similarly, older workers who are already familiar with Word can more easily transfer their knowledge of its menu structure to Excel.\par \par \uc2 Any kind of discrimination against older workers is unacceptable. Organizations simply need to give these employees the opportunity to excel. Office software doesn't have to be the bane of older workers.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Dr. Tams is an associate professor in the department of information technologies at HEC Montreal. He can be reached at reports@wsj.com.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Older Workers Can Keep Up" -- WSJ Dec. 16, 2021)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211129ehbt00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
