\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Latest Bill Would Be Boon to Unions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brody Mullins, Julie Bykowicz and Ted Mann \par \uc2 737 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden's nearly $2 trillion spending bill passed by the House is stocked with benefits for organized labor, including a measure aimed at helping unionized companies win billions of dollars in new green-energy projects.\par \par \uc2 The legislation also has labor-backed measures to raise fines for violations of workplace safety and other rules, expands funding for home-care services provided by union workers and allows union members to write off some of their dues.\par \par \uc2 If approved by the Senate, the bill would be "the most pro-worker bill signed into law in almost a century," said Dan Mauer, the director of government affairs for the Communications Workers of America, which represents workers in the media and telecommunications industries, among others. "The running theme throughout the bill is it gives workers more money, better jobs, more control over their lives."\par \par \uc2 Business lobbyists are lining up in opposition, however, saying the bill spells higher costs that will fall hardest on the smallest companies.\par \par \uc2 "It's ironic that a so-called jobs bill includes an unprecedented attack on small business," said \uc2 Matt Haller\uc2 , the president of the International Franchise Association.\par \par \uc2 The successes by unions in the legislation reflect what labor leaders hope is a political comeback by organized labor after decades of defeats in Washington at the hands of the business lobby. Unions strongly backed the election of Mr. Biden and are among the biggest financial donors to the Democratic Party and its candidates.\par \par \uc2 The bill aims to combine two longstanding goals of the party: Expanding labor unions and fighting climate change.\par \par \uc2 Under the bill, companies that seek federal tax incentives for green-energy public works projects must pay the local prevailing wage and offer worker apprenticeships. Supporters say the measure is designed to put union and nonunion companies on a level playing field in competing for federal contracts.\par \par \uc2 The measures face hurdles in the Senate, where Republicans are expected to ask the Senate parliamentarian to strike several pro-union measures, including a tax benefit for union-made electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Builders and Contractors, a construction trade association representing primarily nonunion commercial and industrial contractors, is mounting a challenge to the measure that would require its members to pay prevailing wages to qualify for federal tax incentives on green projects.\par \par \uc2 "It is an unprecedented expansion of anticompetitive and costly pro-labor policies on private construction projects via the U.S. tax code," said Ben Brubeck, the association's vice president of regulatory, labor and state affairs.\par \par \uc2 Lobbyists for unions succeeded in attaching the pro-labor requirement to $230 billion of the roughly $300 billion in new government tax incentives for green-energy spending.\par \par \uc2 The newer green-energy industry is largely not unionized, according to Tim Schlittner, a spokesman for the AFL-CIO.\par \par \uc2 "We want to make sure that as we fight climate change that good union jobs are at the center of the fight," Mr. Schlittner said.\par \par \uc2 Individual unions also scored wins, including extra tax credits for consumers who buy electric vehicles built in plants staffed by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 . Nonunion auto makers including \uc2 Tesla Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . are lobbying to remove that extra credit.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Mine Workers of America\uc2  secured $500 million for a trust fund that pays medical expenses for coal miners suffering from black lung disease.\par \par \uc2 The NewsGuild-CWA secured a payroll tax credit for news organizations that hire and retain local journalists in the U.S., which benefits both union and nonunion companies.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's bill also gives the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  the authority to levy fines up to $700,000 for willful or repeated violations of workplace safety rules. That is up from $136,532.\par \par \uc2 Labor unions say the current fines haven't been increased in decades and no longer are a disincentive to violating the law.\par \par \uc2 More broadly, Mr. Biden's domestic-spending package includes billions of dollars in federal funds for education and social-safety-net programs championed by labor unions.\par \par \uc2 The legislation calls for $110 billion to launch a universal prekindergarten program, an idea backed by the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It allocates $150 billion to provide seniors and disabled Americans with more medical care in their homes. That provision is supported by the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , which represents home-care workers, among others.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211127ehbr00023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
