\par\par \b \uc2 When Federal Employees Telecommute, Why Are Agencies in D.C.?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Faith Bottum \par \uc2 813 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The metropolitan area of Detroit has around 1.7 million square feet of vacant office space. Surely there's room for the \uc2 Federal Highway Administration\uc2  in all that. With only 41,000 people, Pine Bluff, Ark., has around 41,000 square feet of vacant commercial space. Couldn't the headquarters of, say, the \uc2 U.S. Department of Agriculture\uc2 's Rural Business-Cooperative Service be squeezed in there somewhere?\par \par \uc2 Detroit has been in decline for decades, losing 65% of its population since 1950, and Pine Bluff is often named high on lists of declining towns. So why can't the federal government move some of its operations to those cities?\par \par \uc2 The answer is that it's against the law. A 1947 U.S. Code provision requires "all offices attached to the seat of government" to be "exercised in the District of Columbia." Congress would need to approve any plan to move federal agencies out of Washington.\par \par \uc2 The idea of relocating federal agencies was interesting when it was proposed in the 1990s -- and when it was proposed again in 2018 and 2019. But the redistribution of federal offices seems more than interesting now. It has become pressing -- and more possible -- in the post-Covid world.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is clearly against the idea. In July 2019, amid much opposition from government workers unions, the Trump administration (using congressional approval to reorganize the Interior Department) set in motion a plan to move the \uc2 Bureau of Land Management\uc2  to Grand Junction, Colo. Further ideas were floated for the \uc2 USDA\uc2  to relocate two research agencies to Kansas City, Mo., and Interior to transfer the \uc2 U.S. Geological Survey\uc2  to Denver.\par \par \uc2 This month, Interior Secretary \uc2 Deb Haaland\uc2  declared the BLM project a failure and announced that the bureau will move back to Washington. And maybe the project actually was a failure. The BLM saw 287 employees either retire or leave the agency rather than relocate, and of the 41 who agreed to move, only three ended up in Grand Junction.\par \par \uc2 Critics insist that this attrition is what Mr. Trump wanted all along, but some Democrats favored a Western BLM headquarters, which \uc2 Colorado Sen. Michael Bennet\uc2  called a "positive development." And surely there exist nonideological reasons to want to halt Washington's bloat and aid declining American cities.\par \par \uc2 Federal agencies employ at least 140,000 workers in Washington, with around 70,000 each in Maryland and Virginia suburbs, where Congress has gradually allowed some to spread. About 45% of those employees were listed as eligible for telework in September 2021 data from the Office of Personnel Management. At some agencies, such as the Education Department and the \uc2 National Aeronautics and Space Administration\uc2 , nearly everyone is authorized to telecommute. More than 90% of Energy and Interior department employees are.\par \par \uc2 Agencies that need to work closely with elected officials -- the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2 , for example, and the State Department -- obviously should remain near the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  and White House. But why exactly is the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  in Washington? Or the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  in the suburb of Bethesda, Md.? Or the \uc2 National Science Foundation\uc2  in Alexandria, Va.?\par \par \uc2 In 2019, \uc2 Sen. Joni Ernst\uc2  introduced an act that called for complete repeal of the 1947 provision in the federal code. Sens. Josh Hawley and Marsha Blackburn added a proposal to move the headquarters of 10 federal agencies out of Washington. Both efforts failed, and support from \uc2 Andrew Yang\uc2 , the eccentric 2020 Democratic presidential candidate, gained them little traction.\par \par \uc2 But the appeal has grown with the effects of the pandemic. It makes little sense to have Zoom conferences only between offices on K Street and M Street. Over the internet, Indianapolis is as close as Dupont Circle. A federal agency would make a big difference to Detroit. Federal employees would materially aid Pine Bluff -- just as they would help Spokane, Wash.; Rapid City, S.D.; Sioux City, Iowa; Dayton, Ohio; and Savannah, Ga.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, spreading out the American bureaucratic complex would benefit democracy. Too many executive agencies become politicized simply by their presence in the Beltway hothouse. Too much federalism is lost in the interlocking directorates and alphabet soup of Washington's political culture. Decentralization would protect the nation against first-strike attacks, making the government harder to decapitate.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic has demonstrated that spreading government out is possible. Talent has already been scattered across the country because of the pandemic, and if Washington doesn't need to be the center of everything, then Washington shouldn't be the center of everything.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bottum is the Joseph Rago Memorial Fellow at the Journal.\par \par \uc2 (See related letters: "Letters to the Editor: To Move or Not to Move Agencies Out of D.C.?" -- WSJ Dec. 1, 2021)\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Stop the Spread of Agencies -- WSJ Dec. 4, 2021)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211127ehbr0001k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
