\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Minimum Wage Rises For Federal Contractors\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Harrison \par \uc2 463 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Federal contractors, including janitors, security guards, child-care workers who provide services to the government, will soon be paid a $15-an-hour minimum wage, the Labor Department said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Starting Jan. 30, all new contracts or contract extensions will require the higher minimum wage, which will be indexed to inflation, the department said in announcing it had finalized a rule President Biden called for earlier this year. The rule would raise the minimum wage for contractors from $10.95 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Labor Department officials say about 327,000 contractors could see higher wages under the rule. The government employs a total of around five million contractors, according to Paul Light, a professor at New York University.\par \par \uc2 "Federal contract workers are essential workers and are critical to the federal government," said Labor Secretary Marty Walsh. "It's a step in the right direction."\par \par \uc2 The $15 hourly wage will also apply to outfitters and guides on federal land and to workers with disabilities, some of whom can currently be paid less than the minimum wage, the department said.\par \par \uc2 The rule also eliminates the tipped minimum wage for federal contractors by 2024. The tipped minimum wage allows employers, such as those working in food service, to be paid less than the federal minimum wage if workers make up the difference through tips.\par \par \uc2 Business groups and Republican lawmakers have criticized the administration's efforts to raise the minimum wage for federal contractors.\par \par \uc2 Rep. Virginia Foxx\uc2  of North Carolina, the top Republican on the House Education and Labor Committee, said the rule would make it difficult for small businesses to compete for contracts. "With months of forced shutdowns, inflation, a supply chain crisis and a labor shortage, many job creators are trying to keep their heads above water," she said Monday. "This move by the Biden administration will mean many never catch their breath."\par \par \uc2 At least seven states and the District of Columbia either have a $15 minimum wage requirement already or will in the next few years. Some large employers such as \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Target Corp\uc2 . have also set a $15 wage floor.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has called for Congress to raise the federal minimum wage, which applies to private-sector employers, to $15 an hour from $7.25 an hour. The nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  said raising the federal minimum wage for all workers to $15 an hour by 2025 could deliver raises for 27 million workers but would cost 1.4 million Americans their jobs.\par \par \uc2 The rule announced Monday is one of several regulatory changes the Biden administration has proposed to raise worker pay. Another proposal would limit the tipped minimum wage for all workers, not just those under federal contracts.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211123ehbn0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
