\par\par \b \uc2 Deere Puts Effort Into Rebuilding Inventory\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 715 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 November 2021\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The financial toll of a 35-day worker strike at \uc2 Deere & Co\uc2 . will become clearer this week as the farm and construction equipment manufacturer reports quarterly results and its profit outlook for next year.\par \par \uc2 The company accelerated toward full production as soon as the strike ended late Wednesday. Some workers reported for overnight shifts hours after the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  announced that the company's third offer had been accepted by union-represented employees.\par \par \uc2 Deere resolved the strike as it heads into what is traditionally the high season for farm equipment sales, as farmers make purchases ahead of the growing season. With many corn and soybean producers flush with cash from higher commodity prices, dealers are anticipating heavy business by year-end. Deere started its early-ordering program in August for next year's equipment models.\par \par \uc2 "In the next 45 days, there will be lots of equipment swapped," said Rob Shelton, an account manager for \uc2 Greenway Equipment Inc\uc2 ., a Deere dealer in Arkansas. "Two of our busiest months are in December and January."\par \par \uc2 The five-week strike by more than 10,000 \uc2 UAW\uc2  members was the first against the Moline, Ill., company in 35 years. Production of tractors, harvesting combines, crop sprayers, construction wheel-loaders and other equipment slowed significantly during that period. Nonunion employees and supervisors manning plants shipped equipment that was mostly completed before the walkout.\par \par \uc2 The strike, which straddled the end of the company's fiscal fourth quarter in October and the start of the first quarter of fiscal 2022 in November, likely sliced Deere's production by 10% to 15% in its fourth and first quarters, according to \uc2 William Blair & Co\uc2 . To pay for the cost of the new six-year contract, analysts expect Deere to continue to raise prices and increase production from its plants.\par \par \uc2 Deere declined to comment ahead of reporting quarterly results on Wednesday that are expected to include the initial net-income forecast for 2022.\par \par \uc2 Continued strong demand for farm and construction machinery should help Deere backfill lost production and sales caused by the walkout.\par \par \uc2 The Agriculture Department in September projected net farm income would surge 20% this year to $113 billion, the highest since 2013.\par \par \uc2 Farmers typically spend extra income on equipment because they can deduct those purchases from their federal income taxes as business investments.\par \par \uc2 U.S. retail-sales volume of high-horsepower tractors and combines industrywide are up 23% and 24%, respectively, this year through October from a year earlier, according to the \uc2 Association of Equipment Manufacturers\uc2 , an industry trade group.\par \par \uc2 The strike tempered expectations for Deere's fourth quarter, as the consensus estimate for earnings per share slipped to $3.87 from $4.08 in late September, according to FactSet. Analysts anticipate equipment sales of $10.5 billion, which would be a 20% increase from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Deere's shares rose 2.5% to $349.16 over the past month, compared with a 3% increase in the broad market S&P 500 index during the same period.\par \par \uc2 Low inventories of new and used farm equipment in North America and supply-chain disruptions have kept rival equipment makers from increasing their own production to chip away at Deere's leading share of the high-horsepower farm- equipment market, according to analysts.\par \par \uc2 They also said the tight market gives Deere the ability to charge higher prices, at least in the short term, to recover some of its higher labor expenses from the new contract, which Deere said will cost it $3.5 billion over the course of the contract.\par \par \uc2 Labor accounts for about 15% of Deere's overall cost of goods sold, analysts said. The 10% pay raise and $8,500 bonus that each \uc2 UAW\uc2  member will receive and raises awarded to nonunion employees as well are expected to cost Deere about $235 million in the first year of the contract, said Stephen Volkmann, an analyst for Jefferies Research Services. He estimated that the higher cost will shave nearly 1 percentage point off Deere's operating margin.\par \par \uc2 That profit, Mr. Volkmann said, could easily be recovered by higher prices or greater output at Deere factories. Deere increased prices on large farm equipment by about 8% during its just-concluded fiscal year, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020211123ehbn00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
